WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.410 --> 00:00:08.780
You kinda go outside and
notice a very different world

00:00:08.780 --> 00:00:09.970
around you these days.

00:00:09.970 --> 00:00:12.683
A place that's much
quieter and pretty weird.

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:16.290
In this episode I wanted
to explore the idea

00:00:16.290 --> 00:00:20.410
of what this lack of activity looks like

00:00:20.410 --> 00:00:23.850
on a planetary scale and
how the world has changed

00:00:23.850 --> 00:00:26.210
since the virus spread across it.

00:00:26.210 --> 00:00:28.880
And so we are gonna talk to Will Marshall

00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:31.060
who's the CEO of Planet Labs.

00:00:31.060 --> 00:00:33.110
It's a company that has hundreds

00:00:33.110 --> 00:00:35.913
of imaging satellites
surrounding the planet.

00:00:37.270 --> 00:00:40.430
We're also gonna talk to an
Open Source Image Analyst

00:00:40.430 --> 00:00:42.980
named Allison Puccioni to get her take

00:00:42.980 --> 00:00:45.933
on what these images teach
us about what's happening.

00:00:48.700 --> 00:00:51.900
And with that, let's go talk to Will.

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:05.890
Tell me a little bit about who you are

00:01:05.890 --> 00:01:07.650
just for people who don't know Planet

00:01:07.650 --> 00:01:09.320
and what you guys do.

00:01:09.320 --> 00:01:10.153
Sure.

00:01:10.153 --> 00:01:12.850
Planet does Earth imaging satellites

00:01:12.850 --> 00:01:15.693
and analytics on that imagery.

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:20.340
We have about 150 satellites in orbit.

00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:24.380
One set that images the
entire landmass once per day.

00:01:24.380 --> 00:01:28.210
So, really frequent imagery,
as well as 15 satellites

00:01:28.210 --> 00:01:31.400
that delegated to higher
resolution that can zoom

00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:34.660
in on any particular target
of interest that we have.

00:01:34.660 --> 00:01:37.720
As the virus spread across
the globe and everybody went

00:01:37.720 --> 00:01:40.280
on lockdown, you guys
pretty quickly started

00:01:40.280 --> 00:01:42.190
to post some of these images

00:01:42.190 --> 00:01:45.910
of what the world looks
like when it goes quiet.

00:01:45.910 --> 00:01:49.630
What jumped out at you
as things slowed down?

00:01:49.630 --> 00:01:52.200
Well, the very first set of images,

00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:56.080
we were asked to take some
imagery in Wuhan

00:01:56.080 --> 00:01:59.630
and just to see how
dramatic the change was.

00:01:59.630 --> 00:02:03.270
From cars on the roads and on the bridges,

00:02:03.270 --> 00:02:05.233
to suddenly just gone.

00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:09.680
Then as we look around
the rest of the world

00:02:09.680 --> 00:02:14.253
from Disney to Mecca,
people were just gone.

00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:18.280
You were talking about
people asking for images.

00:02:18.280 --> 00:02:22.100
I mean, who has come to
you in this type of crisis

00:02:22.100 --> 00:02:24.510
and what are they looking for?

00:02:24.510 --> 00:02:27.200
Yeah, well the first
folks that were asking

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:29.250
for the data were the news media

00:02:29.250 --> 00:02:30.830
who were just trying to understand,

00:02:30.830 --> 00:02:31.920
especially when they couldn't

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:34.320
send journalists there
anymore, what's going on?

00:02:37.070 --> 00:02:39.060
But then it was quickly followed

00:02:39.060 --> 00:02:42.030
by governments who were
interested in whether

00:02:42.030 --> 00:02:43.570
or not there was anything
that could be done

00:02:43.570 --> 00:02:44.820
for emergency response.

00:02:44.820 --> 00:02:47.830
So, for example, the US
government reached out

00:02:47.830 --> 00:02:50.500
and asked if we could
help monitor some cities

00:02:50.500 --> 00:02:54.050
across the US for their relief operations.

00:02:54.050 --> 00:02:56.490
And, you guys, I think you were deemed

00:02:56.490 --> 00:02:59.130
an essential business during all of this.

00:02:59.130 --> 00:03:00.680
As I understand it, I
mean you've been able

00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:04.340
to keep manufacturing,
but then, more broadly,

00:03:04.340 --> 00:03:07.350
it looks like some of the
rocket factories have had

00:03:07.350 --> 00:03:09.240
to slow down a little bit.

00:03:09.240 --> 00:03:11.810
I mean, what has been
the knock-on effect just

00:03:11.810 --> 00:03:13.330
to the space industry in general,

00:03:13.330 --> 00:03:15.520
whether it's its launches or satellites?

00:03:15.520 --> 00:03:18.380
I would say that space is pretty resilient

00:03:18.380 --> 00:03:19.410
for a couple of reasons.

00:03:19.410 --> 00:03:22.370
Firstly, it's built very resiliently.

00:03:22.370 --> 00:03:24.600
I mean it's a very expensive
business and so on,

00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:28.230
people are very careful with
those triple redundant systems

00:03:28.230 --> 00:03:31.470
and backups and supply chain management.

00:03:31.470 --> 00:03:34.960
And so, our company like
many other space companies

00:03:34.960 --> 00:03:37.470
are pretty robust to weather a storm.

00:03:37.470 --> 00:03:40.550
So, for example, we operate
all of our satellites remotely.

00:03:40.550 --> 00:03:42.470
We have remote mission control systems

00:03:42.470 --> 00:03:45.520
that our folks can do from home.

00:03:45.520 --> 00:03:46.960
All of that stuff is still working.

00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:48.270
The only thing that we have to do

00:03:48.270 --> 00:03:50.000
in person is the manufacturing.

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:51.120
We've got special permission

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:52.890
so we can continue to do that.

00:03:52.890 --> 00:03:54.940
And then, I think a second part of it is

00:03:54.940 --> 00:03:57.440
that the space sector is very connected

00:03:57.440 --> 00:03:58.970
with government needs,

00:03:58.970 --> 00:04:00.820
whether that's in the
communication satellites

00:04:00.820 --> 00:04:03.210
or in earth observation or what have you.

00:04:03.210 --> 00:04:06.160
And governments in these sorts
of times tend to spend more,

00:04:06.160 --> 00:04:08.240
not less, both to stimulate the economy

00:04:08.240 --> 00:04:11.523
and because they're very
necessary for emergency response.

00:04:15.500 --> 00:04:17.690
I know that you think pretty broadly

00:04:17.690 --> 00:04:20.020
about humanity in general.

00:04:20.020 --> 00:04:22.420
You know, what has this
experience been like?

00:04:22.420 --> 00:04:25.220
What, I don't know,
what's struck you the most

00:04:25.220 --> 00:04:27.140
over the past couple of weeks?

00:04:27.140 --> 00:04:30.870
Well, look, this is an extraordinary time.

00:04:30.870 --> 00:04:32.790
It's almost like a real-time test

00:04:32.790 --> 00:04:34.663
of the competence of government.

00:04:36.550 --> 00:04:39.760
And that's very interesting
to see how it evolves

00:04:39.760 --> 00:04:41.510
where countries like South Korea

00:04:41.510 --> 00:04:45.700
get on with testing very
quickly and lock down early.

00:04:45.700 --> 00:04:47.600
They're going to do
better than the countries

00:04:47.600 --> 00:04:49.000
that fail to do those things.

00:04:49.000 --> 00:04:51.310
And, that's not an
experiment you want to run

00:04:51.310 --> 00:04:54.120
at the expense of human
lives, but we are seeing it.

00:04:54.120 --> 00:04:56.080
But then there are some silver lining

00:04:56.080 --> 00:04:58.940
and I think that it's
important in any disaster

00:04:58.940 --> 00:05:01.173
to look for those opportunities.

00:05:02.580 --> 00:05:05.520
It's like humanity is testing its reflex

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:07.340
of emergency response muscle.

00:05:07.340 --> 00:05:09.240
And a bit of strength
in that is a good idea

00:05:09.240 --> 00:05:11.710
because we're going to see
more emergencies come up.

00:05:11.710 --> 00:05:12.810
Because of climate change,

00:05:12.810 --> 00:05:16.720
we're going to see many more
big scale disasters happen

00:05:16.720 --> 00:05:19.290
whether floods or hurricanes or fires.

00:05:19.290 --> 00:05:21.420
And we're going to need to
be able to respond to them.

00:05:21.420 --> 00:05:24.480
This is a test of how humans
can change their behavior,

00:05:24.480 --> 00:05:27.620
like stopping flying, like doing
much more virtual meetings,

00:05:27.620 --> 00:05:31.150
like potentiality consuming less.

00:05:31.150 --> 00:05:33.300
I do hope that through this,

00:05:33.300 --> 00:05:37.160
humanity will be a little more
focused on the environment.

00:05:37.160 --> 00:05:40.860
And our imagery is, of course,
extremely important for that.

00:05:47.330 --> 00:05:50.045
You're an open source image analyst.

00:05:50.045 --> 00:05:53.102
Can you tell us what that
means, and what you do?

00:05:53.102 --> 00:05:54.753
So, there are a number
of different satellites

00:05:54.753 --> 00:05:58.223
in space that any one
of us can get access to.

00:06:01.470 --> 00:06:03.427
You can call up somebody
with a credit card and say,

00:06:03.427 --> 00:06:05.040
"Hey, I want an image of this,"

00:06:05.040 --> 00:06:06.210
and I look at those pictures,

00:06:06.210 --> 00:06:08.560
and I try to find
interesting patterns of life.

00:06:09.420 --> 00:06:12.490
I've always been on the
more security end of that.

00:06:12.490 --> 00:06:16.760
Whether that means looking
for naval weapons in China,

00:06:16.760 --> 00:06:19.520
or low intensity conflict in Africa,

00:06:19.520 --> 00:06:22.173
or even refugee displacement.

00:06:24.390 --> 00:06:26.080
These images used to be kind of rare

00:06:26.080 --> 00:06:27.700
and precious and prized,

00:06:27.700 --> 00:06:29.354
and mostly government.
They were totally

00:06:29.354 --> 00:06:30.533
classified, yeah.

00:06:30.533 --> 00:06:35.500
These things weren't
available at this quantity

00:06:35.500 --> 00:06:37.513
until honestly in the last five years.

00:06:38.570 --> 00:06:40.490
But now you have all sorts of countries

00:06:40.490 --> 00:06:43.340
that are building their
own satellite industry.

00:06:43.340 --> 00:06:46.500
And we are able to benefit
from this emergence

00:06:46.500 --> 00:06:49.740
of transparency like we've
never been able to do before.

00:06:49.740 --> 00:06:52.240
And so, when something like,

00:06:52.240 --> 00:06:54.740
when there's this big global story,

00:06:54.740 --> 00:06:56.640
is there anything that's popped out to you

00:06:56.640 --> 00:06:58.260
during the last couple of weeks?

00:06:58.260 --> 00:07:00.920
Yeah, there have been, I
think a couple of the images

00:07:00.920 --> 00:07:04.510
over Iran that showed what
I think the Washington Post

00:07:04.510 --> 00:07:08.720
called burial pits, of a
number of people being buried.

00:07:08.720 --> 00:07:10.600
But it is over a cemetery,

00:07:10.600 --> 00:07:12.590
and they are not really mass graves

00:07:12.590 --> 00:07:14.310
and they're not really burial pits.

00:07:14.310 --> 00:07:16.620
What we're seeing is basically a country

00:07:16.620 --> 00:07:20.620
that had people die in
February from a pandemic,

00:07:20.620 --> 00:07:22.130
and they hastily buried them,

00:07:22.130 --> 00:07:23.980
but they buried them individually

00:07:23.980 --> 00:07:26.840
and they had individually marked graves.

00:07:26.840 --> 00:07:28.370
It sounds like part of
what you're saying is

00:07:28.370 --> 00:07:30.875
either some part of the media

00:07:30.875 --> 00:07:32.600
was either sensationalizing this,

00:07:32.600 --> 00:07:34.860
or just didn't fully
understand the context

00:07:34.860 --> 00:07:35.980
of what they were seeing,

00:07:35.980 --> 00:07:38.810
or like the right way to talk about this?

00:07:38.810 --> 00:07:40.310
I would just say tread carefully,

00:07:40.310 --> 00:07:42.270
because we're looking at a catastrophe.

00:07:42.270 --> 00:07:43.750
We're looking at a pandemic,

00:07:43.750 --> 00:07:45.340
and these are people who don't

00:07:45.340 --> 00:07:46.860
have a disregard for their dead.

00:07:46.860 --> 00:07:49.570
They are there on the
worst days of their lives.

00:07:49.570 --> 00:07:51.560
In this particular case, is this somewhere

00:07:51.560 --> 00:07:53.810
that's looked at on a daily basis?

00:07:53.810 --> 00:07:57.480
So, when we need the resolution
or the clarity, I guess,

00:07:57.480 --> 00:08:00.593
to see graves being dug,

00:08:01.450 --> 00:08:03.070
we need a high-resolution satellite.

00:08:03.070 --> 00:08:05.940
There used to be literally
five high resolution satellites

00:08:05.940 --> 00:08:09.350
in the early 2010s, and
now I've lost count.

00:08:09.350 --> 00:08:11.120
I think there are 36, 37.

00:08:11.120 --> 00:08:13.060
So at any given point
in the earth's surface,

00:08:13.060 --> 00:08:15.280
we used to be able to see twice a year,

00:08:15.280 --> 00:08:18.240
and now we can probably
see roughly twice a week

00:08:18.240 --> 00:08:21.170
or maybe five or six times a month.

00:08:21.170 --> 00:08:22.610
And showing sort of what
the world looks like

00:08:22.610 --> 00:08:25.470
when it comes to a standstill
in all these places.

00:08:25.470 --> 00:08:27.570
Did anything jump out at you?

00:08:27.570 --> 00:08:29.760
Just 'cause you have such
a keen eye for this stuff?

00:08:29.760 --> 00:08:30.593
Yeah.

00:08:30.593 --> 00:08:32.770
I'm actually working with
a number of organizations

00:08:32.770 --> 00:08:34.740
who are trying to do a COVID response

00:08:34.740 --> 00:08:36.890
using satellite imagery.

00:08:36.890 --> 00:08:39.310
The analytics teams at places like Planet,

00:08:39.310 --> 00:08:41.140
they are doing metrics.

00:08:41.140 --> 00:08:43.710
Are people populating certain areas?

00:08:43.710 --> 00:08:45.180
Really interesting details

00:08:45.180 --> 00:08:48.870
that can augment municipal
information that we already have.

00:08:48.870 --> 00:08:50.530
And then, you can actually do things

00:08:50.530 --> 00:08:52.550
that allow people to learn

00:08:52.550 --> 00:08:55.830
whether people are
sheltering in place or not.

00:08:55.830 --> 00:08:58.910
One is cell data records,
or tracking cell phone data,

00:08:58.910 --> 00:09:01.720
which has been a very
effective tool in showing

00:09:01.720 --> 00:09:04.600
how people are traveling from their home.

00:09:04.600 --> 00:09:08.280
In certain countries, the cell
phone records are tracking

00:09:08.280 --> 00:09:12.340
to hospitals, versus grocery stores.

00:09:12.340 --> 00:09:15.300
So, if you see that in one county,

00:09:15.300 --> 00:09:16.820
people are still going
to the grocery store

00:09:16.820 --> 00:09:18.880
more than the hospital,
then maybe that means

00:09:18.880 --> 00:09:21.332
there hasn't been a big outbreak there.

00:09:21.332 --> 00:09:22.165
Yeah.

00:09:22.165 --> 00:09:24.830
And yet, we have to ask
ourselves the ethical question,

00:09:24.830 --> 00:09:26.922
is this okay right now?

00:09:26.922 --> 00:09:30.440
If we can track people and potentially

00:09:30.440 --> 00:09:33.700
put shelter in place
orders in certain regions

00:09:33.700 --> 00:09:36.490
that could have the
potential to save lives,

00:09:36.490 --> 00:09:41.490
is that more important than
someone's autonomy or secrecy?

00:09:43.760 --> 00:09:45.110
We're in this interesting moment

00:09:45.110 --> 00:09:47.430
when there's a lot more
truth about the world

00:09:47.430 --> 00:09:49.690
that can be gleaned through data.

00:09:49.690 --> 00:09:52.410
I mean, just as someone who's
watched this virus play out,

00:09:52.410 --> 00:09:53.390
what have you--

00:09:53.390 --> 00:09:55.400
Well, I mean you bring up
a really interesting point.

00:09:55.400 --> 00:09:57.070
When the Washington Post, for instance,

00:09:57.070 --> 00:09:59.320
among a lot of other news organizations

00:09:59.320 --> 00:10:04.320
published the satellite imagery
over the cemetery in Iran,

00:10:04.580 --> 00:10:07.210
there was a very strong slant on

00:10:07.210 --> 00:10:10.260
Iran has been obfuscating
the actual numbers

00:10:10.260 --> 00:10:13.340
of people who had
perished from coronavirus.

00:10:13.340 --> 00:10:14.820
And this satellite industry shows

00:10:14.820 --> 00:10:17.150
that there's a fair amount more people

00:10:17.150 --> 00:10:19.203
that have perished from coronavirus.

00:10:23.420 --> 00:10:27.070
Satellite Imagery does hold
people's leaders, in fact,

00:10:27.070 --> 00:10:30.020
feet to the fire,
because it's possible now

00:10:30.020 --> 00:10:33.840
for civilians, and for
academic institutions to check.

00:10:33.840 --> 00:10:36.200
So, it would, at the very least,

00:10:36.200 --> 00:10:38.430
force people to be more transparent

00:10:38.430 --> 00:10:40.992
about what's actually
going on in their country.

